comments.text:
I was initially very low, at 5%, but increased recently. I agree with the comment below, that democratic government might scrap or modify oil deals that have been made, which might make the US hesitate before pushing for elections.

However, Rodríguez has publicly committed to elections at the UN. Domestic and international pressure is mounting. So there is a rhetorical commitment, but every indicator points to "someday" pledge rather than a real announcement.&#x20;

I will stay slightly above the community forecast for the rest of the question timeframe.

* I think the chances for an announcement of a presidential election is low, as CNE (National Electoral Council) and Judicial Reforms are missing, both of which are seen as vital and indispensable steps by opposition and international observers before holding presidential elections. Without these two reforms, any presidential election would lack international and institutional legitimacy. While they aren't legally required prior the presidential elections, though experts view them as intermediate step.
* Secondly the US is busy in signing energy deals and prioritizing economic agreements and everything else can wait. The primary objective is to open the dormant oil industry of Venezuela, the US already has friendly interim government there, who is dancing to their tunes, this likely means that the election isn't the top priority.&#x20;
* The US often sought to install democratic governments abroad, as we have seen this taking place in the past in countries like Afghanistan, Vietnam, Iraq and recently in Iran. There ain't any immediate threat of rise of communism or socialism in Venezuela, N.Maduro has been dismantled.
* Trump himself said earlier this month that Venezuela is not yet ready for elections. Apart from this, the key variable we'd watch is whether the CNE publishes a formal call for an election, rather than relying on a mere statement from interim government or any local body.
  &#x20;
